</math>. It&rsquo;s given that in triangle <math alttext="upper A upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, point <math alttext="upper D"><mi>D</mi>
</math> on side <math alttext="upper A upper B"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
</mrow>
</math> is connected by a line segment with point <math alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi>
</math> on side <math alttext="upper A upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math> such that line segment <math alttext="upper D upper E"><mrow>
	<mi>D</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
</mrow>
</math> is parallel to side <math alttext="upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. It follows that parallel segments <math alttext="upper D upper E"><mrow>
	<mi>D</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math> are intersected by sides <math alttext="upper A upper B"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="upper A upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. If two parallel segments are intersected by a third segment, corresponding angles are congruent. Thus, corresponding angles <math alttext="upper C"><mi>C</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="upper A upper E upper D"><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>D</mi></math> are congruent and corresponding angles <math alttext="upper B"><mi>B</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="upper A upper D upper E"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>D</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
</mrow>
</math> are congruent. Since triangle <math alttext="upper A upper D upper E"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>D</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
</mrow>
</math> has two angles that are each congruent to an angle in triangle <math alttext="upper A upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, triangle <math alttext="upper A upper D upper E"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>D</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
</mrow>
</math> is similar to triangle <math alttext="upper A upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math> by the angle-angle similarity postulate, where side <math alttext="upper D upper E"><mrow>
	<mi>D</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
</mrow>
</math> corresponds to side <math alttext="upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, and side <math alttext="upper A upper E"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
</mrow>
</math> corresponds to side <math alttext="upper A upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. Since the lengths of corresponding sides in similar triangles are proportional, it follows that <math alttext="StartFraction upper D upper E Over upper B upper C EndFraction equals StartFraction upper A upper E Over upper A upper C EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. Since point <math alttext="upper E"><mi>E</mi>
</math> lies on side <math alttext="upper A upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, <math alttext="upper A upper E plus upper C upper E equals upper A upper C"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mi>A</mi>
			<mi>E</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mrow>
			<mi>C</mi>
			<mi>E</mi>
		</mrow>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mi>A</mi>
		<mi>C</mi>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>. It's given that <math alttext="upper C upper E equals 2 upper A upper E"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mi>C</mi>
		<mi>E</mi>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mn>2</mn>
		<mi>A</mi>
		<mi>E</mi>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>. Substituting <math alttext="2 upper A upper E"><mrow>
	<mn>2</mn>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
</mrow>
</math> for <math alttext="upper C upper E"><mrow>
	<mi>C</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
</mrow>
</math> in the equation <math alttext="upper A upper E plus upper C upper E equals upper A upper C"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mi>A</mi>
			<mi>E</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>+</mo>
		<mrow>
			<mi>C</mi>
			<mi>E</mi>
		</mrow>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mi>A</mi>
		<mi>C</mi>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math> yields <math alttext="upper A upper E plus 2 upper A upper E equals upper A upper C"><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math>, or <math alttext="3 upper A upper E equals upper A upper C"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mn>3</mn>
		<mi>A</mi>
		<mi>E</mi>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mi>A</mi>
		<mi>C</mi>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>. It&rsquo;s given that <math alttext="upper B upper C equals 162"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mi>B</mi>
		<mi>C</mi>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>162</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Substituting <math alttext="162"><mn>162</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="upper B upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>B</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="3 upper A upper E"><mrow>
	<mn>3</mn>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
</mrow>
</math> for <math alttext="upper A upper C"><mrow>
	<mi>A</mi>
	<mi>C</mi>
</mrow>
</math> in the equation <math alttext="StartFraction upper D upper E Over upper B upper C EndFraction equals StartFraction upper A upper E Over upper A upper C EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext="StartFraction upper D upper E Over 162 EndFraction equals StartFraction upper A upper E Over 3 upper A upper E EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>162</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext="StartFraction upper D upper E Over 162 EndFraction equals one third"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>162</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext="162"><mn>162</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="upper D upper E equals 54"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mi>D</mi>
		<mi>E</mi>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>54</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Thus, the length of line segment <math alttext="upper D upper E"><mrow>
	<mi>D</mi>
	<mi>E</mi>
</mrow>
</math> is <math alttext="54"><mn>54</mn>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b1e1c2f5","external_id":"ece15db3-71cb-4f5a-8d30-df4238c90943","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"4aa16904-c269-42e7-97de-96f8a6d00312","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.B.","skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In right triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, angle <math alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi>\n</math> is the right angle and <math alttext=\"upper B upper C equals 162\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>162</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Point <math alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi>\n</math> on side <math alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is connected by a line segment with point <math alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi>\n</math> on side <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> such that line segment <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is parallel to side <math alttext=\"upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper C upper E equals 2 upper A upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. What is the length of line segment <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["54"],"correct_answer":["54"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"54\"><mn>54</mn>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that in triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, point <math alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi>\n</math> on side <math alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is connected by a line segment with point <math alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi>\n</math> on side <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> such that line segment <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is parallel to side <math alttext=\"upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. It follows that parallel segments <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> are intersected by sides <math alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. If two parallel segments are intersected by a third segment, corresponding angles are congruent. Thus, corresponding angles <math alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper A upper E upper D\"><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>D</mi></math> are congruent and corresponding angles <math alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper A upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> are congruent. Since triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> has two angles that are each congruent to an angle in triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is similar to triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> by the angle-angle similarity postulate, where side <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> corresponds to side <math alttext=\"upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, and side <math alttext=\"upper A upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> corresponds to side <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Since the lengths of corresponding sides in similar triangles are proportional, it follows that <math alttext=\"StartFraction upper D upper E Over upper B upper C EndFraction equals StartFraction upper A upper E Over upper A upper C EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. Since point <math alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi>\n</math> lies on side <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"upper A upper E plus upper C upper E equals upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. It's given that <math alttext=\"upper C upper E equals 2 upper A upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"2 upper A upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper C upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> in the equation <math alttext=\"upper A upper E plus upper C upper E equals upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"upper A upper E plus 2 upper A upper E equals upper A upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math>, or <math alttext=\"3 upper A upper E equals upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that <math alttext=\"upper B upper C equals 162\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>162</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"162\"><mn>162</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"3 upper A upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> in the equation <math alttext=\"StartFraction upper D upper E Over upper B upper C EndFraction equals StartFraction upper A upper E Over upper A upper C EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext=\"StartFraction upper D upper E Over 162 EndFraction equals StartFraction upper A upper E Over 3 upper A upper E EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>162</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"StartFraction upper D upper E Over 162 EndFraction equals one third\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>162</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"162\"><mn>162</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"upper D upper E equals 54\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>54</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Thus, the length of line segment <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"54\"><mn>54</mn>\n</math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1730147357407,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"b1e1c2f5","skill_cd":"S.B.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Lines, angles, and triangles","createDate":1730147357407,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"ece15db3-71cb-4f5a-8d30-df4238c90943","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"4aa16904-c269-42e7-97de-96f8a6d00312","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"answerOptions":[],"externalid":"ece15db3-71cb-4f5a-8d30-df4238c90943","keys":["54"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"54\"><mn>54</mn>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that in triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, point <math alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi>\n</math> on side <math alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is connected by a line segment with point <math alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi>\n</math> on side <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> such that line segment <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is parallel to side <math alttext=\"upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. It follows that parallel segments <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> are intersected by sides <math alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. If two parallel segments are intersected by a third segment, corresponding angles are congruent. Thus, corresponding angles <math alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper A upper E upper D\"><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>D</mi></math> are congruent and corresponding angles <math alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper A upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> are congruent. Since triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> has two angles that are each congruent to an angle in triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is similar to triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> by the angle-angle similarity postulate, where side <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> corresponds to side <math alttext=\"upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, and side <math alttext=\"upper A upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> corresponds to side <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Since the lengths of corresponding sides in similar triangles are proportional, it follows that <math alttext=\"StartFraction upper D upper E Over upper B upper C EndFraction equals StartFraction upper A upper E Over upper A upper C EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. Since point <math alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi>\n</math> lies on side <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, <math alttext=\"upper A upper E plus upper C upper E equals upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. It's given that <math alttext=\"upper C upper E equals 2 upper A upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"2 upper A upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper C upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> in the equation <math alttext=\"upper A upper E plus upper C upper E equals upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"upper A upper E plus 2 upper A upper E equals upper A upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math>, or <math alttext=\"3 upper A upper E equals upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that <math alttext=\"upper B upper C equals 162\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>162</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"162\"><mn>162</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"3 upper A upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> in the equation <math alttext=\"StartFraction upper D upper E Over upper B upper C EndFraction equals StartFraction upper A upper E Over upper A upper C EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext=\"StartFraction upper D upper E Over 162 EndFraction equals StartFraction upper A upper E Over 3 upper A upper E EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>162</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"StartFraction upper D upper E Over 162 EndFraction equals one third\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow><mn>162</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"162\"><mn>162</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"upper D upper E equals 54\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>54</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Thus, the length of line segment <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"54\"><mn>54</mn>\n</math>.</p>","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In right triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, angle <math alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi>\n</math> is the right angle and <math alttext=\"upper B upper C equals 162\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>162</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Point <math alttext=\"upper D\"><mi>D</mi>\n</math> on side <math alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is connected by a line segment with point <math alttext=\"upper E\"><mi>E</mi>\n</math> on side <math alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> such that line segment <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is parallel to side <math alttext=\"upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper C upper E equals 2 upper A upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mi>C</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t\t<mi>E</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. What is the length of line segment <math alttext=\"upper D upper E\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>D</mi>\n\t<mi>E</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>?</p>","type":"spr","correct_answer":["54"]},"createDate":1730147357407,"updateDate":1730147357407}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147357407, 1730147357407),
