</math>?</p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="5"><mn>5</mn></math>. It's given that in the <em>xy</em>-plane, the graph of the equation&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis x minus 3 right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y minus 5 right parenthesis squared equals 9"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></math> is a circle. It&rsquo;s also given that the point <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 6 comma c right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>)</mo></math>, where&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="c"><mi>c</mi></math> is a constant, lies on this circle. It follows that the ordered pair&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 6 comma c right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>)</mo></math> makes the equation&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis x minus 3 right parenthesis squared plus left parenthesis y minus 5 right parenthesis squared equals 9"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></math> true. Substituting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="6"><mn>6</mn></math> for&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x"><mi>x</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="c"><mi>c</mi></math> for&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="y"><mi>y</mi></math> in this e